1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,370
The first light bulb that really worked lasted thirteen and a half hours.

2
00:00:04,670 --> 00:00:13,109
The bulb you'd buy today gives the same light for about fifteen percent of the electricity of the bulb most Americans grew up with.

3
00:00:13,409 --> 00:00:17,357
Here's how we got from one to the other, and what that's worth on your bill.

4
00:00:17,657 --> 00:00:27,482
In this video, I'll walk through the story of the light bulb in six moments, from a lab in New Jersey to the rules that will shape the bulbs of twenty twenty-eight.

5
00:00:27,782 --> 00:00:37,107
At each step, I'll show you what one bulb costs to run for a year, so by the end you'll know exactly how much the bulbs in your house are worth changing.

6
00:00:37,408 --> 00:00:42,965
Moment one: October twenty-second, eighteen seventy-nine, Menlo Park, New Jersey.

7
00:00:43,265 --> 00:00:50,479
Thomas Edison's team had already spent about a year and a half testing materials for the glowing part of a lamp, the filament.

8
00:00:50,779 --> 00:00:54,293
The filament is the whole trick of an incandescent bulb.

9
00:00:54,594 --> 00:01:02,579
Electricity flows through it, it gets so hot that it glows, and the glass bulb keeps the air out so it doesn't simply burn up.

10
00:01:02,879 --> 00:01:10,927
That morning, they tried a piece of cotton thread, carbonized, which means baked in a sealed chamber until only carbon was left.

11
00:01:11,227 --> 00:01:14,030
They sealed it inside a glass bulb and turned it on.

12
00:01:14,330 --> 00:01:16,908
It kept glowing for thirteen and a half hours.

13
00:01:17,208 --> 00:01:27,526
The Edison Papers project at Rutgers University, which keeps the lab's records, calls it the first successful experiment with a high-resistance carbon filament.

14
00:01:27,826 --> 00:01:32,857
Then they pushed more power through it, the bulb overheated and cracked about an hour later.

15
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:37,123
But for the first time, they had a lamp with real commercial promise.

16
00:01:37,423 --> 00:01:43,412
Ten days later, on November first, Edison filed a patent application for the carbon filament.

17
00:01:43,712 --> 00:01:51,165
And on December thirty-first, he held the first public demonstration of his electric lighting system at Menlo Park.

18
00:01:51,465 --> 00:01:58,397
But a lamp is useless without electricity to run it, and in eighteen seventy-nine almost no home had any.

19
00:01:58,697 --> 00:02:01,282
So Edison's team kept improving the filament.

20
00:02:01,582 --> 00:02:08,347
In July eighteen eighty, they tested bamboo, and bamboo filaments became standard in Edison lamps.

21
00:02:08,647 --> 00:02:11,702
Moment two: September fourth, eighteen eighty-two.

22
00:02:12,002 --> 00:02:21,027
At two fifty-seven Pearl Street, in lower Manhattan, Edison's generating station began supplying electricity to paying customers.

23
00:02:21,327 --> 00:02:26,500
On the first day, there were about four hundred lamps, for about eighty-five customers.

24
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:32,396
Within a year, there were about ten thousand lamps, serving five hundred thirteen customers.

25
00:02:32,696 --> 00:02:38,626
That's the moment the light bulb stopped being an experiment and became something you pay for every month.

26
00:02:38,926 --> 00:02:40,402
Which brings us to the bill.

27
00:02:40,702 --> 00:02:45,246
Before we jump forward, one thing you need to know to read a light bulb box.

28
00:02:45,546 --> 00:02:49,532
For most of the twentieth century, people bought bulbs by watts.

29
00:02:49,832 --> 00:02:53,960
But watts measure the energy a bulb uses, not how bright it is.

30
00:02:54,260 --> 00:02:56,199
Brightness is measured in lumens.

31
00:02:56,499 --> 00:03:01,546
A traditional sixty-watt incandescent bulb gives about eight hundred lumens.

32
00:03:01,846 --> 00:03:05,297
That's the brightness most of us picture as a normal bulb.

33
00:03:05,597 --> 00:03:10,584
So in this video, every bulb I compare gives the same eight hundred lumens.

34
00:03:10,884 --> 00:03:14,378
The only question is how many watts it takes to get there.

35
00:03:14,678 --> 00:03:25,731
And to put a price on it, I'll assume each bulb is on three hours a day, at the national average electricity price of eighteen point one nine cents per kilowatt-hour.

36
00:03:26,031 --> 00:03:29,453
Here's the math, so you can do it for any bulb in your house.

37
00:03:29,753 --> 00:03:35,712
Take the watts, multiply by the hours it's on in a year, and divide by one thousand.

38
00:03:36,012 --> 00:03:37,698
That gives you kilowatt-hours.

39
00:03:37,998 --> 00:03:39,843
Then multiply by your price.

40
00:03:40,143 --> 00:03:43,955
Three hours a day is one thousand ninety-five hours a year.

41
00:03:44,255 --> 00:03:50,023
So a sixty-watt bulb uses about sixty-five point seven kilowatt-hours a year.

42
00:03:50,323 --> 00:03:56,480
At that rate, the classic sixty-watt bulb costs about eleven dollars and ninety-five cents a year.

43
00:03:56,780 --> 00:03:57,828
Just one bulb.

44
00:03:58,128 --> 00:04:00,319
Now jump forward more than a century.

45
00:04:00,619 --> 00:04:07,255
Light bulbs got cheaper and more common, but the basic idea, a hot glowing filament, stayed the same.

46
00:04:07,555 --> 00:04:11,068
Then came the compact fluorescent bulb, the spiral one.

47
00:04:11,368 --> 00:04:14,548
Moment three: January first, two thousand six.

48
00:04:14,848 --> 00:04:22,970
A federal standard required the common spiral bulbs of fifteen watts or more to give at least sixty lumens for every watt.

49
00:04:23,270 --> 00:04:33,732
By twenty fifteen, according to the government's household energy survey, thirty-two percent of American homes used fluorescent bulbs for all or most of their lighting.

50
00:04:34,032 --> 00:04:38,673
Thirty-one percent still used mostly incandescent or halogen bulbs.

51
00:04:38,973 --> 00:04:39,615
And LED?

52
00:04:39,915 --> 00:04:41,047
Just four percent.

53
00:04:41,347 --> 00:04:43,783
Moment four: the old bulb gets a limit.

54
00:04:44,083 --> 00:04:52,387
Between twenty twelve and twenty fourteen, a federal standard capped how many watts a regular bulb could use for each level of brightness.

55
00:04:52,687 --> 00:04:53,808
It came in steps.

56
00:04:54,108 --> 00:05:06,180
The brightest bulbs went first: from January first, twenty twelve, a bulb giving fourteen hundred ninety to twenty-six hundred lumens could use no more than seventy-two watts.

57
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:10,765
In twenty thirteen, the next size down was capped at fifty-three watts.

58
00:05:11,065 --> 00:05:18,311
For an eight-hundred-lumen bulb, the limit became forty-three watts, starting January first, twenty fourteen.

59
00:05:18,611 --> 00:05:24,185
So a new eight-hundred-lumen bulb on store shelves had to use forty-three watts or less.

60
00:05:24,485 --> 00:05:31,690
Running cost? About eight dollars and fifty-six cents a year, instead of eleven dollars and ninety-five cents.

61
00:05:31,990 --> 00:05:37,113
Better, but still almost five times what a typical LED uses for the same light.

62
00:05:37,413 --> 00:05:39,690
Moment five: the LED takes over.

63
00:05:39,990 --> 00:05:47,729
By twenty twenty, forty-seven percent of American homes used LED bulbs for all or most of their indoor lighting.

64
00:05:48,029 --> 00:05:54,232
That's about fifty-seven point seven million homes, up from four percent just five years earlier.

65
00:05:54,532 --> 00:05:57,234
Mostly fluorescent homes fell to twelve percent.

66
00:05:57,534 --> 00:06:01,297
Mostly incandescent or halogen homes, to fifteen percent.

67
00:06:01,597 --> 00:06:12,421
Then, starting July twenty-fifth, twenty twenty-two, the federal government banned the sale of any general service bulb that gives less than forty-five lumens per watt.

68
00:06:12,721 --> 00:06:18,016
For an eight-hundred-lumen bulb, that's a limit of about seventeen point eight watts.

69
00:06:18,316 --> 00:06:20,351
The forty-three-watt bulb was out.

70
00:06:20,651 --> 00:06:23,653
And a typical LED does much better than the limit.

71
00:06:23,953 --> 00:06:31,259
The Federal Trade Commission says you can find an eight-hundred-lumen LED that uses only about nine watts.

72
00:06:31,559 --> 00:06:38,789
Nine watts, three hours a day, at the national average price: about one dollar and seventy-nine cents a year.

73
00:06:39,089 --> 00:06:40,468
And the shift kept going.

74
00:06:40,768 --> 00:06:48,935
By twenty twenty-four, the EIA says thirty-seven percent of households used LED bulbs for all of their indoor lighting.

75
00:06:49,235 --> 00:06:50,892
Moment six hasn't happened yet.

76
00:06:51,192 --> 00:06:59,093
For general service bulbs made on or after July twenty-fifth, twenty twenty-eight, the federal standard gets much tighter.

77
00:06:59,393 --> 00:07:08,532
For a regular eight-hundred-lumen LED bulb, the new formula works out to about one hundred twenty-four point three lumens per watt.

78
00:07:08,832 --> 00:07:15,646
That's a maximum of about six point four watts, and about one dollar and twenty-eight cents a year to run.

79
00:07:15,946 --> 00:07:18,663
The rule applies to bulbs made from that date on.

80
00:07:18,963 --> 00:07:22,052
Nobody comes to take the bulbs already in your lamps.

81
00:07:22,352 --> 00:07:24,824
It just changes what you'll find on the shelf.

82
00:07:25,124 --> 00:07:31,157
So let's line up the whole story, one eight-hundred-lumen bulb, three hours a day, for a year.

83
00:07:31,457 --> 00:07:36,254
The classic sixty-watt incandescent: eleven dollars and ninety-five cents.

84
00:07:36,554 --> 00:07:41,685
The forty-three-watt bulb from twenty fourteen: eight dollars and fifty-six cents.

85
00:07:41,985 --> 00:07:47,997
The twenty twenty-two limit, seventeen point eight watts: three dollars and fifty-four cents.

86
00:07:48,297 --> 00:07:52,126
A nine-watt LED: one dollar and seventy-nine cents.

87
00:07:52,426 --> 00:07:56,337
And the twenty twenty-eight limit: one dollar and twenty-eight cents.

88
00:07:56,637 --> 00:08:03,749
Swap one sixty-watt bulb for a nine-watt LED, and you save about ten dollars and sixteen cents a year.

89
00:08:04,049 --> 00:08:07,927
Swap ten of them, and that's about one hundred two dollars a year.

90
00:08:08,227 --> 00:08:09,927
Where you live changes the number.

91
00:08:10,227 --> 00:08:19,882
In Washington State, where electricity averages fourteen point four two cents, that one swap saves about eight dollars and five cents a year.

92
00:08:20,182 --> 00:08:26,447
In California, at thirty-two point five eight cents, about eighteen dollars and nineteen cents.

93
00:08:26,747 --> 00:08:28,355
And there's still room to do that.

94
00:08:28,655 --> 00:08:37,006
In twenty twenty, about seventy-five point two million American homes still had at least one incandescent or halogen bulb.

95
00:08:37,306 --> 00:08:39,465
That's sixty-one percent of all homes.

96
00:08:39,765 --> 00:08:42,193
The biggest single win is often outside.

97
00:08:42,493 --> 00:08:50,017
About thirteen point six two million homes left at least one incandescent or halogen bulb on outdoors all night.

98
00:08:50,317 --> 00:08:57,298
If a sixty-watt porch light runs twelve hours a night, that's about forty-seven dollars and eighty cents a year.

99
00:08:57,598 --> 00:09:03,180
A nine-watt LED doing the same job: about seven dollars and seventeen cents.

100
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:07,630
That's about forty dollars and sixty-three cents saved, from one bulb.

101
00:09:07,930 --> 00:09:09,259
One honest caveat.

102
00:09:09,559 --> 00:09:12,860
Your savings depend on how long each bulb is really on.

103
00:09:13,160 --> 00:09:17,669
A bulb in a closet you open twice a day will never save ten dollars.

104
00:09:17,969 --> 00:09:19,990
The one in your kitchen might save more.

105
00:09:20,290 --> 00:09:22,490
So start with the lights you use the most.

106
00:09:22,790 --> 00:09:24,007
So that's the story.

107
00:09:24,307 --> 00:09:29,103
A cotton thread that glowed for thirteen and a half hours in eighteen seventy-nine.

108
00:09:29,403 --> 00:09:32,757
Four hundred lamps on Pearl Street in eighteen eighty-two.

109
00:09:33,057 --> 00:09:41,330
Then decades of the same hot bulb, until rules and LEDs cut the electricity for the same light to about fifteen percent.

110
00:09:41,630 --> 00:09:46,979
For one sixty-watt bulb you swap, that's about ten dollars and sixteen cents a year.

111
00:09:47,279 --> 00:09:53,979
If you want to see which appliances in your home cost the most to run, the full ranking is right here on the channel.
